1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,658
An impersonator can copy a creator's photos and biography while changing just the username.

2
00:00:06,658 --> 00:00:11,645
MirrorCheck helps turn that suspicion into evidence a reviewer can inspect.

3
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:16,498
This is our working, unofficial Instagram-style web prototype.

4
00:00:16,498 --> 00:00:20,472
These two fictional accounts share a name, biography,

5
00:00:20,472 --> 00:00:24,446
avatar and post images, but have different usernames.

6
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:34,101
From the suspected copy, I choose Report account, then Pretending to be someone else.

7
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:38,354
I attach screenshots of the original and suspected profile.

8
00:00:38,354 --> 00:00:42,131
PaddleOCR fills editable fields, and I review the

9
00:00:42,131 --> 00:00:45,908
extracted text against the screenshots before submitting.

10
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:56,930
Before any image reaches Meta AI, Presidio identifies the example email and phone.

11
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:00,571
The server permanently covers both contact lines.

12
00:01:00,571 --> 00:01:04,457
These previews are the actual processed images prepared for Meta,

13
00:01:04,457 --> 00:01:08,343
with matching hashes for the evidence saved for review.

14
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:16,876
Meta Muse Spark analyzes the processed screenshots through

15
00:01:16,876 --> 00:01:21,403
our backend and returns structured similarities, differences and limitations.

16
00:01:21,403 --> 00:01:26,498
Here it identifies reused profile content and recommends further review.

17
00:01:26,498 --> 00:01:30,452
It explicitly says screenshots cannot establish ownership.

18
00:01:30,452 --> 00:01:34,147
This is a real API response from the recorded run.

19
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:40,938
Local models add separate signals: a text-reuse comparison between

20
00:01:40,938 --> 00:01:45,525
the two profiles, and an auxiliary profile spam-pattern score.

21
00:01:45,525 --> 00:01:49,723
Image deduplication also finds matching photo regions.

22
00:01:49,723 --> 00:01:55,022
These scores are evidence signals, not accuracy claims or proof of identity.

23
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,528
The report remains Under review.

24
00:01:58,528 --> 00:02:02,346
Our profile model excludes avatar presence, so changing

25
00:02:02,346 --> 00:02:06,164
only a default-avatar flag cannot change its score.

26
00:02:06,164 --> 00:02:11,748
The pair model uses synthetic training data, a limitation shown to the reviewer.

27
00:02:14,350 --> 00:02:19,019
In the moderator view, I inspect the processed images and

28
00:02:19,019 --> 00:02:23,688
analysis, then explicitly select Confirm impersonation for this fictional case.

29
00:02:23,688 --> 00:02:27,954
The AI recommendation alone cannot perform this transition.

30
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:36,009
The profile now displays Impersonation confirmed.

31
00:02:36,009 --> 00:02:40,710
Reloading preserves the warning because the decision is stored on the server.

32
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:47,666
This demonstrates the complete report-to-review workflow; it does not modify the real Instagram service.

33
00:02:50,343 --> 00:02:55,386
MirrorCheck makes suspicious copies easier to review while reducing contact exposure.

34
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:59,651
Next, we need consented real-world evaluation and production hardening.
